{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVXMkBYjte7AuGw9ihmL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GCnqZZiXSm3Jur7mntRiGz","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cat > reference_query_plan.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"How few comparisons have to be dropped or flipped to change the top-1 or top-k set of a Bradley-Terry leaderboard. The size is measured with a one-step influence (AMIP-style) search verified by exact refits, and is compared across crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena 140k), expert and LLM-judge annotations (MT-Bench human and GPT-4 pairs), and sports results (NBA seasons), with synthetic and permutation controls.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood (ridge-regularized, ties as half wins) fitted to pairwise comparison data\",\n    \"Approximate Maximum Influence Perturbation (AMIP): first-order, influence-function ranking of rows by their effect on the leader-challenger margin\",\n    \"Exponential + binary prefix search with exact refits to verify the smallest perturbation set that changes the ranking\",\n    \"Two perturbation types (dropping rows, flipping outcomes) and two targets (top-1, top-k set)\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Random-subset perturbation baseline (the run reports the log2 size ratio vs random)\",\n    \"Influence functions and leave-one-out / infinitesimal-jackknife approximations\",\n    \"Data-dropping robustness metrics for statistical conclusions (Broderick et al.)\",\n    \"Adversarial manipulation and vote rigging of pairwise rank aggregation and crowdsourced LLM leaderboards\",\n    \"Statistical uncertainty for Bradley-Terry rankings (bootstrap / asymptotic confidence intervals) and top-K ranking theory\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Chatbot Arena human preference votes (arena-human-preference-140k)\",\n    \"MT-Bench human expert judgments and GPT-4 pairwise judgments\",\n    \"FiveThirtyEight NBA historical game records (2014 and 2015 seasons)\",\n    \"Synthetic Bradley-Terry control and permutation control\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Changing the perturbation model from dropping comparisons to flipping outcomes: flip sets are consistently smaller than drop sets, e.g. Arena top-1 46 flips vs 62 drops\",\n    \"Changing the target from top-1 to the top-k set: on Arena and NBA, top-5 set membership is more fragile than top-1 (Arena 21 vs 62 drops; NBA 1 vs 7 or 16 drops)\",\n    \"Same model responses, different evaluation design: on MT-Bench, human expert and GPT-4 judges give different fragility\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Large crowdsourced leaderboards can be extremely fragile in relative terms: dropping about 0.05% of Arena-140k votes changes the top-1\",\n    \"Small expert or LLM-judge datasets need a much larger fraction to change the top-1 (about 2.7% for MT-Bench human, about 6% for MT-Bench GPT-4); sports seasons fall in between (about 0.9%)\",\n    \"Influence-guided sets are about 16x smaller than random subsets (mean log2 ratio about 4.0), so the gap between worst-case and average-case sensitivity is large\",\n    \"Fragility depends on the margin between adjacent ranks and on the comparison graph design, not only on dataset size, so a dataset-size-normalized fraction is needed for comparisons across designs\",\n    \"The refined search variants (category-count relinearized knapsack) were never evaluated because of infrastructure failures; the reported sizes are upper bounds from the AMIP search\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The search used in every reported number is AMIP-style. This query retrieves Broderick et al. and follow-ups on exact or certified versions of the drop-data metric, and on where the first-order approximation fails, which is needed to describe our sizes as upper bounds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"influence functions infinitesimal jackknife approximate leave-one-out generalized linear models\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The one-step influence score is a Newton/influence approximation for a logistic-type GLM, as in Koh & Liang. This query grounds the approximation and its accuracy, including the known gaps when many points are removed together.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model maximum likelihood estimation existence MM algorithm paired comparisons\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The estimator is a ridge-regularized Bradley-Terry MLE, so we need the classic model, Zermelo/Ford existence conditions (relevant when dropped rows disconnect the graph) and Hunter's MM fitting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"top-K ranking from pairwise comparisons sample complexity spectral MLE\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Our top-k-set targets and the margin dependence of fragility link to theory on when top-K sets can be identified from noisy comparisons (Rank Centrality, spectral MLE). Reviewers will expect this literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Flipping or dropping comparisons to change a ranking is a data-poisoning attack on rank aggregation. This is the closest comparator literature to the flip/drop sets, alongside the starter vote-rigging paper.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena crowdsourced pairwise human preference LLM evaluation leaderboard\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Arena-140k is the main crowdsourced dataset. This query grounds the platform design, how its rankings are computed (BT with bootstrap CIs) and the known critiques of it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"LLM-as-a-judge agreement with human expert pairwise judgments biases\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"MT-Bench human vs GPT-4 judgments are our second evaluation design, and their fragility differs. Work on judge bias and on human-judge agreement explains why the two can differ.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Elo rating predictive accuracy professional sports paired comparison\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The NBA seasons are the sports-design control, which has a dense, balanced schedule. This query grounds rating systems for sports results and their link to Bradley-Terry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"confidence intervals uncertainty quantification Bradley-Terry ranking bootstrap\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A key result is that worst-case perturbation sizes are tiny on Arena even though the usual CIs are narrow there. To contrast sampling-uncertainty CIs with data-dropping fragility, we need the literature on ranking inference.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any query copying the research question title, e.g. 'stability of pairwise comparison leaderboards' or 'sensitivity of Bradley-Terry rankings to data perturbation' (the task forbids retrieving the prior published study or pages citing it)\",\n    \"Queries naming 'dropping a handful of preferences' / Huang et al. follow-ups or 'who cites' style queries that could surface the forbidden paper or derivative discussions\",\n    \"Generic filler: 'machine learning benchmarking', 'robust optimization', 'scientific method', 'paper writing'\",\n    \"Chronology or search-process queries (cycle names, knapsack/relinearized variant names, harness/infra failures)\",\n    \"Queries for the untested category-count knapsack variant as if it were a validated contribution\",\n    \"Generic LLM leaderboard survey queries with no link to pairwise comparison, influence or manipulation\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\nEOF\npython3 -m json.tool reference_query_plan.json >/dev/null && echo ok","description":"Write reference query plan JSON"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2691,"cache_read_input_tokens":38203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2691},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99ef2d0e-c81b-4a3c-ae36-c2fd72b6b289","uuid":"7acc9cce-f3ef-4831-b45a-4b052c131918","timestamp":"2026-10-28T23:45:03.605Z","request_id":"req_011CgVXMjFV7aQfSfuf7y68i"}
